  "paradigm_name": "Trust Game (Investment Game)",
  "reference": "Berg, J., Dickhaut, J., & McCabe, K. (1995). Trust, reciprocity, and social history. *Experimental Economics*, 2(2), 122‑142.",
  "known_results": "In typical laboratory settings participants send roughly 30–50 % of their endowment and trustees return about 30–40 % of the amount they receive (i.e., ~⅓ of the tripled amount). Average send ≈3 of 10 dollars; average return ≈0.3·(3X)."
}

{
  "recognized": true,
  "paradigm_name": "Trust Game (also called Investment Game)",
  "reference": "Berg, N., Dickhaut, J., & McCabe, K. (1995). Trust, Reciprocity, and Social History. *American Economic Review*, 85(2), 349‑354.",
  "known_results": "In typical lab settings with a 3× multiplier, senders (trustors) transfer roughly 30‑40% of their endowment (≈3‑4 out of 10), and receivers (trustees) return about 30‑50% of the multiplied amount (≈2‑3 out of 3X). The average return rate is around 0.5 of the amount received, yielding modest but positive gains for both players."
}

{
  "recognized": true,
  "paradigm_name": "Trust Game (Investment Game)",
  "reference": "Berg, J., Dickhaut, J., & McCabe, K. (1995). Trust, Reciprocity, and Social History. Econometrica, 63(4), 800‑827.",
  "known_results": "Senders typically transfer about 30–40% of their 10‑point endowment (≈3–4 points). Trustees return roughly 30–50% of the amount they receive (≈0.3–0.5 of 3X, i.e., about 2–3 points on average)."
}

{
  "recognized": true,
  "paradigm_name": "Trust (Investment) Game",
  "reference": "Berg, J., Dickhaut, J., & McCabe, K. (1995). Trust, Reciprocity, and Social Welfare: Experimental Evidence. The Economic Journal, 105(485), 123–142.",
  "known_results": "In typical laboratory settings, senders (investors) transfer about 40‑50% of their endowment (≈4–5 out of 10) and receivers (trustees) return roughly 30‑40% of the tripled amount (≈0.3–0.4 of 3X). The game yields positive reciprocity and higher total payoffs than the Nash equilibrium prediction of zero transfer."
}
